<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tar River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pamlico River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tar River flows south-east across the piedmont and coastal plain of North Carolina; below the town of Washington its estuary is called the Pamlico River, which opens into Pamlico Sound. Before European settlement its valley lay within the lands of the Tuscarora. Early colonial settlements on the Pamlico, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>, founded in 1705, were among the first targets of the Tuscarora attack of 1711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tar-river.jpg" alt="Tar River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Department of Agriculture · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tar_River_next_to_East_Carolina_University_in_Greenville,_North_Carolina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Tar River is approximately 215 miles (346 km) long and flows generally south-east across north-east North Carolina to an estuary of Pamlico Sound. It becomes the tidal Pamlico River where it passes under the U.S. Highway 17 bridge in Washington. Louisburg, Rocky Mount, Tarboro and Greenville stand along its course, and its basin, with that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuse-river">Neuse River</a>, is home to species found nowhere else.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>North Carolina began as a naval stores colony: the longleaf pines of the coastal plain gave masts for the British Navy, and their pitch was made into tar for caulking ships. The river takes its name from its use as a major route for barges carrying tar to the sea. Before the Civil War the North Carolina Legislature funded dams and locks to allow navigation for much of the year between Tarboro, Greenville and Washington. The village of Old Sparta was once an important river port but declined in the 20th century.</p><p>One account of the name comes from the Civil War. As the Confederates prepared to leave Washington in March 1862, they sent squads along the river to destroy naval stores so that Union troops could not take them; at Taft's store more than 1,000 barrels of turpentine and tar were emptied into the river. Union prisoners of war who bathed there three months later, on their way to be exchanged, came out smeared with tar.</p><p>Hurricane Floyd in 1999 brought the worst flooding on the river, exceeding 500-year flood levels along its lower stretches and cresting 24 feet above flood stage; at Greenville it reached 29.74 ft. Hurricane Matthew in 2016 raised it up to 24.5 feet above flood stage for as long as three days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Overfishing, pollution, river alterations and habitat loss have reduced the numbers of several native species, among them the endangered Tar River Spinymussel and the Neuse River Waterdog, a salamander listed as threatened in 2021. The environmental organisation Sound Rivers maintains fifteen camping platforms and campsites along the Tar-Pamlico Water Trail.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tar River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tuscarora lived on the coastal plain of North Carolina along the Roanoke, <b>Tar</b> and Neuse rivers, an Iroquoian-speaking people among Algonquian and Siouan neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuscarora-nation">Tuscarora Nation</a></li></ul></section>
